+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+
+Ext4 truncate/write/fsync microbenchmark optimizations
+======================================================
+
+Motivation
+----------
+
+The ``bm_min_rocks_fcntl_pread64_0_0`` benchmark repeatedly truncates a
+regular file, writes roughly 2 MiB through buffered I/O, calls ``fdatasync()``,
+and then performs small read-side operations.  The collected perf data shows
+that the scalability limit is not ``fcntl()`` or ``pread64()``.  The hot paths
+are ext4 buffered write/page-cache setup, full-file truncate invalidation,
+JBD2 buffer release checks, ext4 writeback, bio submission, and block flushes.
+
+This branch keeps the changes deliberately small.  It does not change ext4's
+on-disk format, journaling mode selection, write ordering, or fsync policy.
+Instead it removes avoidable work from common clean data-folio paths hit by the
+benchmark.
+
+Changes
+-------
+
+* ``ext4_release_folio()`` now sends ordered/writeback regular-file data folios
+  directly to ``try_to_free_buffers()``.  Data-journaled folios still use
+  ``jbd2_journal_try_to_free_buffers()``.
+
+* ``block_invalidate_folio()`` now has an early release path for full-folio
+  invalidation when the folio is clean, not under writeback, and all attached
+  buffers are clean and unlocked.  If the filesystem refuses to release the
+  folio, the existing invalidate/discard path runs unchanged.
+
+* ``ext4_bio_write_folio()`` batches cgroup writeback owner accounting once per
+  folio for the number of bytes actually submitted, instead of charging once
+  per buffer.
+
+Correctness and safety
+----------------------
+
+The ext4 release fast path is limited to folios for which ext4 does not journal
+regular file data.  ``ext4_should_journal_data()`` keeps data-journal mode on
+the existing JBD2 path, and ``folio_test_checked()`` still prevents releasing a
+folio carrying dirty journaled data.  In ordered and writeback data modes,
+``ext4_invalidate_folio()`` already relies on the invariant that regular data
+buffers are not owned by JBD2.  The new branch uses the same invariant to avoid
+JBD2 journal-head locking during release.
+
+The full-folio invalidate fast path only handles complete folio invalidations.
+It first verifies that the folio is clean, not under writeback, and that no
+buffer is dirty or locked.  It then delegates the actual removal to
+``filemap_release_folio()`` and returns only if that removal succeeds.  If any
+precondition is not met, or if the filesystem-specific release callback says
+the folio cannot be released, the previous buffer discard logic is used.
+
+The writeback accounting batching is byte-equivalent for a folio because
+``wbc_account_cgroup_owner()`` derives ownership from the folio's memory cgroup.
+Several per-buffer charges from the same folio and one charge for their summed
+byte count produce the same owner vote and byte totals.
+
+Expected effect
+---------------
+
+The benchmark's high-parallelism runs spend visible CPU time in
+``block_invalidate_folio()``, ``ext4_release_folio()``, JBD2 journal-head
+handling, and buffer-head allocation/freeing after each truncate.  The first
+two changes reduce work in that exact clean truncate path after the prior
+``fdatasync()`` has pushed data out.  The cgroup accounting change is smaller,
+but removes repeated per-buffer accounting when folios contain multiple
+buffers.
+
+These patches should be validated with the same benchmark using full-system
+perf sampling, lock contention tracing around JBD2 and buffer-head locks, and
+xfstests coverage for ext4 ordered, writeback, and data-journal modes.
